# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Econt delivery (e-Econt JSON API)
#
# SERVER-SIDE ONLY. Never prefix any of these with NEXT_PUBLIC_ — that would
# inline the credentials into the browser bundle. Everything that reads them
# lives under lib/econt/ and starts with `import 'server-only'`.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# fixture = no network, served from lib/econt/fixtures (the default, so a fresh
#           clone works offline and in sandboxes that block *.econt.com).
# live    = talk to ECONT_BASE_URL for real.
ECONT_MODE=fixture

# Test:       https://demo.econt.com/ee/services
# Production: https://ee.econt.com/services
#
# REQUIRED whenever ECONT_MODE=live — there is no default in live mode, on
# purpose. Credentials set with this left blank would otherwise fall back to
# demo and quote demo tariffs as if they were the contract's, silently.
#
# The two environments have SEPARATE accounts: real contract credentials do not
# work against demo, and the demo pair below does not work against production.
# Note also that demo returns a demo tariff — only production returns the rates
# your contract actually charges, so only production prices are safe to show.
ECONT_BASE_URL=https://demo.econt.com/ee/services

# Econt's public demo credentials. Replace with the real ones (from the Econt
# business contract) via the Vercel environment UI — never in this file.
ECONT_USERNAME=iasp-dev
ECONT_PASSWORD=1Asp-dev

# Force a failure in fixture mode to exercise the UI's error branches:
# timeout | auth | validation | upstream | empty
# ECONT_FIXTURE_FAULT=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sender — us. Becomes the shipment's sender.
#
# Required to PRICE OR CREATE a shipment, but deliberately not to read the
# nomenclature: you need to list offices in order to find your own office code,
# so requiring the code first would be circular. City name must be in Bulgarian
# (Cyrillic) and the phone is passed to Econt verbatim, so no spaces.
#
# Where parcels ship FROM is no longer configured here.
#
# It lives in lib/data/dispatch.ts, because it is the shop's own address rather
# than a secret, it changes roughly never, and as eight environment variables a
# forgotten one stayed invisible until a customer reached the delivery quote.
# In code a missing field is a typecheck failure instead.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Econt automat (АПС) parcel limits. Defaults live in lib/econt/constraints.ts;
# override here when Econt changes them, without a deploy of new code.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ECONT_APS_MAX_WEIGHT_KG=20
# ECONT_APS_MAX_L_CM=60
# ECONT_APS_MAX_W_CM=40
# ECONT_APS_MAX_H_CM=40

### Payments (Stripe)

There is deliberately no on/off switch. Card payment is enabled when the secret
and publishable keys are both present and belong to the same Stripe account, so
entering the keys is the whole step. A second flag was one more thing to forget
at launch, and forgetting it produced the worst failure shape available: keys
set, checkout apparently working, webhook returning 503, and every paid order
stuck at `pending` forever.

With no keys the intent route answers "unavailable" and the webhook returns 503,
so a fresh clone behaves exactly like the site did before payments existed —
same reasoning as `ECONT_MODE=fixture` being the default.

```
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_...
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_...
NEXT_PUBLIC_STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=pk_test_...
```

`GET /api/health` reports what a running deployment is still missing, including
whether real cards would be refused. Ask it rather than the dashboard: Vercel
bakes variables into a deployment at build time, so "I set it" and "the running
site has it" are different facts.

**`NEXT_PUBLIC_STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY` is the one deliberate exception to the
rule above that no variable may be `NEXT_PUBLIC_`.** That rule exists because of
the Econt password. A Stripe publishable key is designed to be public and
`loadStripe()` needs it in the browser, so it is prefixed on purpose. Do not
"fix" it, and do not add the secret key alongside it.

**There are three different `whsec_` values, and they are not interchangeable.**
`stripe listen` prints one for local forwarding, the test-mode Dashboard endpoint
has another, and the live-mode endpoint a third. Using the wrong one fails
signature verification on every event, which looks exactly like an outage.

### Database (Supabase)

Server-side only. The service role bypasses RLS, and `public.orders` holds
customer names, phone numbers and addresses.

```
SUPABASE_URL=https://<ref>.supabase.co
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=
```

**Do not add `NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY`.** Its absence is the safeguard:
with no publishable database credential in the bundle, no client-side path to the
orders table can be created by accident. `public.orders` has RLS enabled with
zero policies, so the service role is the only way in, and `lib/supabase/admin.ts`
is the only module that holds it.
